                <h2>First: How Many Creatives Do You Actually Need?</h2>
<p>The conservative answer is <strong>at least 15-20 pieces</strong>, but that number alone is meaningless. The real question is: can these creatives cover different "failure modes"?</p>
<p>TikTok's algorithm is essentially rapid elimination. Which stage a creative dies at determines what type you need to supplement. If 80% of your creatives get stuck at "5,000 impressions, 0 conversions," the issue is lack of authenticity. If they die at "CTR 3%, but CVR near zero," the hook works but product value isn't clear. These two failures require completely different creative strategies.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Q1: How to Allocate Creative Types?</h2>
<p><strong>Verdict: Don't bet on a single type—diversify risk.</strong></p>
<p>Recommended ratio (by count):</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Creative Type</th>
<th>Ratio</th>
<th>Best For</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>UGC/Lifestyle</td>
<td>40-50%</td>
<td>Trust building, comment engagement</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Product Demo + Voiceover</td>
<td>30-40%</td>
<td>Information delivery, scaling</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Montage/Static Image</td>
<td>15-20%</td>
<td>Quick testing, low cost</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Many teams start with 100% product demos, and users just swipe past. <strong>UGC-style creatives typically have 30-50% higher completion rates</strong> than pure product demos, and this gap directly determines whether you pass the initial traffic pool.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Q2: What's the Best Upload Cadence?</h2>
<p><strong>Verdict: Uploading everything at once is a mistake—batch test with small volumes.</strong></p>
<p>Recommended cadence:<br />
- <strong>Days 1-3</strong>: Upload 3-5 creatives daily, monitor basic CTR<br />
- <strong>Days 4-7</strong>: Keep top performers, kill low performers, add new creatives<br />
- <strong>Day 8+</strong>: Enter normal iteration phase</p>
<p>Uploading 20 creatives at once seems efficient but eliminates the opportunity to dynamically adjust based on early data. One tool app team discovered that <strong>creatives uploaded in 3 batches had 2x the hit rate</strong> compared to one massive upload.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Q3: How to Allocate Test Budget?</h2>
<p><strong>Verdict: Don't budget less than $50/day per creative.</strong></p>
<p>Below this threshold, the algorithm doesn't get enough data samples and may misjudge creative quality. A common mistake: splitting a $1,000 daily budget across 20 creatives ($50 each), then complaining nothing works.</p>
<p>The right approach: <strong>confirm which 2-3 creatives show positive signals first</strong>, then concentrate budget. Specific steps:</p>
<ol>
<li>Allocate $80-100/day per creative initially</li>
<li>After 5,000-10,000 impressions, keep creatives with CVR &gt; 1%</li>
<li>Consolidate budget to TOP 2 creatives and observe stability</li>
</ol>
<hr />
<h2>Q4: When Should You Pull the Plug on Underperforming Creatives?</h2>
<p><strong>Verdict: If no clicks within 3,000 impressions, pull immediately.</strong></p>
<p>TikTok's traffic pool elimination is fast—waiting too long wastes budget. Specific criteria:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>CTR &lt; 0.5%</strong>: Hook completely failed, don't wait</li>
<li><strong>CTR 0.5-1%, CVR 0%</strong>: Possible audience targeting issue, but swap creative to verify</li>
<li><strong>CTR &gt; 1.5%, CVR &gt; 1%</strong>: Run to 30,000-50,000 impressions before scaling</li>
</ul>
<p>A useful rule of thumb: <strong>don't give failed creatives a second chance</strong>. Many advertisers think "maybe the data will improve," but you're usually just wasting time.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Q5: Should You Keep Doubling Down on Winning Creatives?</h2>
<p><strong>Verdict: Scale them, but always test replacement creatives simultaneously.</strong></p>
<p>Winning creatives have a lifecycle. After 2-3 weeks, CTR will decline (creative fatigue), requiring replacement creatives to fill the gap. But many teams panic when winners dip, scramble for new creatives, and waste budget on untested material.</p>
<p>Better strategy: <strong>while your winning creative is running, the next batch is already testing</strong>. Build a creative pipeline so new and winning creatives maintain a rolling state.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Q6: Can You Repurpose Cold Launch Creatives Across Platforms?</h2>
<p><strong>Verdict: Yes, but re-edit and re-voice.</strong></p>
<p>TikTok and Instagram Reels vertical videos can migrate directly, but watch out:</p>
<ul>
<li>Voiceovers/subtitles need adaptation for target market language</li>
<li>TikTok favors conversational, fast-paced content—Meta platforms need slower pacing</li>
<li>Don't test the same creative on both platforms simultaneously, or the algorithm may flag it as duplicate delivery</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>One Core Principle</h2>
<p>Creative pool management is fundamentally a <strong>probability game</strong>, not about shooting one "perfect" creative that goes viral. What you need is: enough creatives to cover different failure modes, rapid testing to identify effective ones, and budget concentration to scale winners.</p>
<p>The biggest cold launch mistake isn't poor creative quality—it's <strong>insufficient volume, slow testing, and lazy refresh</strong>. Get these three things right and new products will scale much faster.</p>
